# RME payment submission idempotency & financial integrity (rule 205)

Supporting hardening after Sprint 2 of DAENGTISIAMS-RME-BILLING-CORRECTION.
It changes **how a payment request is recognised and decided**, never what a
payment is allowed to settle. Rules 191, 196–204 stand unchanged; where a test
pinned the old behaviour this rule names the supersession explicitly (§K).

Evidence: `docs/sprints/fix-rme-payment-submission-idempotency-1.md`.

## A. The two defects this rule closes

01. **F1 — no request identity.** None of the four payment paths
    (`pay`, `allocateControlPayment`, `payControlInvoiceOnly`,
    `allocateVisitPayment`) knew whether a request had been seen before. A
    double click, a browser retry or a resubmitted form recorded a SECOND
    partial payment, because the second request still saw enough remaining
    balance. A replayed full payment was refused as an error instead of being
    recognised as the payment that already happened.
02. **F3 — a decision taken before the lock.** The Kontrol and visit paths read
    the payable summary and capped the amount BEFORE taking the row locks. Under
    the locks an origin or receivable that had stopped being collectable was
    skipped (`continue`) and the rest `min()`-capped: the cashier submitted
    300.000 and 100.000 was recorded (silent truncation), or money meant for one
    invoice landed on the next (silent redistribution).

## B. The payment intent

03. **Server-issued, per render.** `RmePaymentIntent::issue($invoiceId)` returns
    `nonce.signature`: a 32-hex random nonce and an HMAC-SHA256 over
    `rme-payment-intent|v1|<nonce>|<invoice id>` keyed by the application key.
    It is minted when the payment page is rendered and posted back unchanged.
04. **Stateless.** Issuing writes nothing. Nothing is stored until the payment
    commits (§C), so an abandoned page leaves no row behind.
05. **Bound to one invoice.** A token minted for invoice A does not verify for
    invoice B; the HMAC covers the route invoice id.
06. **Grants nothing.** An intent is an identity, never an authority. Every
    permission, branch, hold, version, dispute and correction check runs exactly
    as before; a valid intent only decides "new" versus "seen".
07. **Required, fail-closed.** A missing intent is refused
    (`PAYMENT_INTENT_MISSING`) and a malformed or forged one is refused
    (`PAYMENT_INTENT_INVALID`). There is no fallback that records an
    unidentified payment — a form opened before this release is refused with a
    reload instruction, the rule-191 precedent.
08. **Comparison is constant-time** (`hash_equals`), and an empty application
    key is a configuration error, never an unsigned token.

## C. The submission ledger

09. **`trx_rme_payment_submissions` — one row per COMMITTED submission.** The
    row is inserted inside the same transaction as the payment rows, after them.
    A row exists if and only if the payment committed; a rolled-back payment
    leaves no ghost.
10. **No status column.** There is no "pending" or "failed" state to reconcile,
    expire or clean up.
11. **`UNIQUE(intent_key)` is the backstop.** Two transactions that both pass
    the application check cannot both commit; the loser's unique violation is
    resolved to the winner's result (§D).
12. **Immutable.** The model refuses update and delete; the migration's `down()`
    refuses while rows exist. History is never rewritten to make a replay pass.
13. **PostgreSQL CHECK constraints** pin the shape: path in
    `single|control|visit|control_only`, intent key `^[0-9a-f]{32}$`,
    fingerprint `^[0-9a-f]{64}$`, amount > 0, payment count ≥ 1.
14. **Nothing historical is backfilled.** Payments recorded before this release
    have no submission row and are not given one.
15. **No PII.** The row holds ids, a path, an amount, a fingerprint hash and a
    timestamp — never a name, NIK, reference text or note.

## D. Replay and reuse

16. **Fingerprint.** SHA-256 over a canonical, versioned payload: route invoice,
    cashier, scope, Kontrol-only confirmation, plan, method, amount in cents,
    paid-at normalised to the application timezone, reference, notes, the sorted
    selected receivables, the declared versions and the declared balances in
    cents. Key order and number formatting cannot change it. Free text that is
    not valid UTF-8 is encoded by its exact bytes and the JSON encoding throws on
    failure, so no payload ever collapses onto one constant fingerprint.
17. **Replay = same intent + same cashier + same invoice + same fingerprint.**
    The original committed result is returned and **nothing is written**: no
    payment, no status change, no allocation, no lab candidate.
18. **Reuse = same intent, anything else different.** Refused with
    `PAYMENT_INTENT_REUSED`. It never leaks the original payment to a different
    cashier, and never applies the original intent to a different amount.
19. **Replay is decided twice.** Early in the FormRequest (so a replay is never
    refused by a balance check that the first submission itself made stale) and
    again under the route-invoice lock in the service (so a direct service call
    or a racing duplicate cannot slip past). A unique violation at commit is
    resolved the same way.
20. **Replay respects current authority.** The cashier's authority is
    re-checked under the lock BEFORE the replay is answered: an account that has
    since lost billing permission, branch scope or activity is refused, not
    shown the earlier receipt.
21. **The operator sees the truth.** A replay redirects to the original receipt
    (or the invoice page while it is still partially paid) with: "Pembayaran ini
    sudah tercatat sebelumnya — tidak ada pembayaran baru yang dibuat. No.
    Kwitansi: …". A multi-invoice replay restores the original allocation summary.
22. **Two genuine installments are two intents.** A fresh page render issues a
    fresh intent, so paying an invoice in parts still works; only a repeated
    submission of the SAME rendered form is collapsed.

## E. What the page declares

23. **`payment_plan`** — `single`, `receivables`, `control` or `control_only`,
    chosen by `RmePaymentDeclaredView::planFor()`, the same rule the controller
    renders with.
24. **`invoice_balances[<id>]`** — the remaining balance the page displayed for
    the route invoice and every carry-over parent or outstanding receivable it
    showed, rendered as fixed two-decimal strings and compared in cents.
25. **Neither is authority.** They are the cashier's view, verified against the
    locked rows; the server never pays an invoice because the page named it and
    never trusts a client id as financial authority.

## F. Deciding under the lock

26. **Lock order is unchanged:** the route invoice first, then the Kontrol
    origins in chain order or the selected receivables in FIFO order. No new
    lock is introduced ahead of an existing one — but the WHOLE plan is now
    locked, so two payments of one patient whose pages list each other's
    invoices can deadlock. PostgreSQL aborts one; the payment transaction is
    retried (3 attempts, each re-deciding everything under fresh locks) and,
    if it still conflicts, refused with `PAYMENT_CONCURRENT_RETRY` and a reload
    instruction — never a 500, never a partial write. Any other database error
    keeps its meaning.
26a. **A retried attempt re-reads everything it writes.** Every model a payment
    attempt mutates (the invoices AND their visits) is re-derived inside the
    attempt from the rows it just locked — never captured from outside the
    retried closure. A model a rolled-back attempt already moved to `completed`
    would make the retry skip visit completion and leave the visit
    `cashier_pending` after the payment committed (security review R1).
27. **The payable summary is read inside the transaction**, after the route lock
    and the intent check — never before them, and the controller's pre-lock
    dispatch is only a routing hint.
28. **Every plan invoice is locked.** None is skipped: an origin or receivable
    that is no longer collectable, or belongs to another branch, refuses the
    WHOLE payment with `PAYMENT_PLAN_CHANGED`.
29. **Plan conflict.** A declared plan that no longer matches what the locked
    rows support (a carry-over appeared or disappeared, the origin set changed,
    a displayed receivable vanished) is refused with `PAYMENT_PLAN_CHANGED`.
30. **Balance conflict.** A declared balance that differs from the locked
    remaining balance is `PAYMENT_BALANCE_CHANGED`; a plan invoice with no
    declared balance is `PAYMENT_BALANCE_MISSING`.
31. **The amount cap is computed from the locked balances**, not from the
    pre-lock summary.
32. **Full allocation is asserted in cents.** If the allocation would record
    less than the amount accepted, the payment is refused with
    `PAYMENT_AMOUNT_NOT_ALLOCATED` — money submitted is never silently dropped.
33. **One transaction, all or nothing.** A refusal at any of these steps rolls
    back every allocation of the batch.
34. **Check order is fixed and test-pinned:** authority → replay → collectable →
    rule-191 version → D10 correction hold / OPS-OQ-1 dispute hold → plan →
    balances → amount cap → allocate → full-allocation assertion. A version or
    hold refusal is never masked by a balance refusal.

## G. Audit

35. **Three events, ids only:** `RME_PAYMENT_SUBMISSION_REPLAYED`,
    `RME_PAYMENT_REFUSED_INTENT_REUSED`, `RME_PAYMENT_REFUSED_STALE_BALANCE`.
    Payloads carry invoice, cashier, path, codes and money — never a patient
    name, NIK, reference text or note.
36. **De-duplicated.** One row per invoice, cashier and action in a 15-minute
    window, so a client retrying in a loop cannot flood the trail.
36a. **Never on a client-chosen invoice.** A stale-view refusal can name an id the
    client supplied; it is audited on that invoice only when it is the route
    invoice or one of the same patient at the same branch, otherwise on the
    route invoice without the foreign id.
37. **Audit never decides.** A failed audit write is logged and the refusal or
    replay stands.
38. **Missing and malformed intents are not audited** — they are client or
    staleness errors with no financial meaning; refusing them is enough.

## H. The page

39. **Hidden fields:** `payment_intent`, `payment_plan`, `invoice_balances[...]`
    on the ordinary and the Kontrol-only form.
40. **Double-click guard in the browser** disables the submit button while the
    request is in flight ("Memproses pembayaran…") — a courtesy only. The server
    is the protection; a server-side disabled state is never re-enabled by it.
41. **Stale-view refusals do not restore** the old amount or selection, so a
    cashier cannot resubmit the stale numbers by pressing save again.
42. **Retry guidance** tells the cashier to reload rather than resubmit.

## I. Preserved, unchanged

43. Rule 191 version binding, D10 correction hold, OPS-OQ-1 / 1a / 1b dispute
    policy, the Kontrol-only payment, OD-1 / OD-1a branch isolation,
    `RmeBillingActorGuard`, `RmeWorkingBranchScope`, D1 / D1a / D1c / D1e pilot
    scope, D7, D21, D28. D21c, D21g and D22 stay PENDING.
44. Full-payment-only and partial-payment semantics, receipt numbering, visit
    completion and lab-candidate generation are unchanged; a replay generates no
    second lab candidate.

## J. Never

45. Never deduplicate in JavaScript, Redis or the cache alone — the database
    unique index is the authority.
46. Never return a stored result to a different cashier, invoice or payload.
47. Never skip an invoice of the plan under the lock, never cap silently, never
    redistribute money onto an invoice the cashier did not see.
48. Never create, replay or reverse a real production payment to verify this
    rule; production verification is read-only.

## K. Supersessions

49. `CrossBranchKontrolFinancialOwnershipTest` — the two "even if discovery
    leaked it" cases used to pin silent truncation (300.000 submitted, 100.000
    recorded) as the safe outcome. They now expect a whole refusal
    (`PAYMENT_PLAN_CHANGED`) with no payment anywhere; the branch-isolation
    property they exist for is asserted unchanged.
50. Rule 200 §9 ("a receivable of another branch is skipped") and rule 196 §28
    (the 2026-10-24 audit that named this sprint) carry in-place supersession
    notes. Every race suite that built a payment form by hand now builds it with
    the intent, plan and balances a rendered page carries
    (`rmeDeclaredPaymentForm()`), so its race still reaches the lock it races on.
